    <title>Booking Confirmation</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>${message}</h1>
    <a href="HotelServlet">Back to Available Rooms</a>
</body>
</html>

Step 6: Configure the web.xml File
This XML configuration file maps the HotelServlet to a specific URL pattern.

<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<web-app xmlns="http://java.sun.com/xml/ns/javaee"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://java.sun.com/xml/ns/javaee 
                             http://java.sun.com/xml/ns/javaee/web-app_3_0.xsd"
         version="3.0">
    <servlet>
        <servlet-name>hotelServlet</servlet-name>
        <servlet-class>com.hotelmanagement.HotelServlet</servlet-class>
    </servlet>
    <servlet-mapping>
        <servlet-name>hotelServlet</servlet-name>
        <url-pattern>/HotelServlet</url-pattern>
    </servlet-mapping>
</web-app>


Step 7: Running the Application
1-Compile all the Java files and deploy the application to a servlet container like Tomcat.
2-Run the application by accessing http://localhost:8080/HotelManagementApp/HotelServlet in a browser.

Flow:
1-User visits HotelServlet (via a GET request), which retrieves the available rooms and forwards the request to index.jsp.
2-User selects a room to book by submitting a form.
3-The Servlet processes the booking request, updates room availability, and forwards the result to confirmation.jsp.
4-confirmation.jsp displays the booking confirmation message.


Conclusion:
This basic integration of Servlets and JSPs demonstrates how Servlets can handle the logic 
(getting available rooms, booking rooms) while JSPs are responsible for presenting dynamic 
content to the user. This is a simple hotel management system that you can expand with 
additional features like customer management, payment processing, etc.



